@@ -51,7 +51,7 @@ Tested with GLM-5.3 on NVIDIA's API (structured output on). A model without JSON
 - In the text box, the type is large enough to read at full speed with the phone at arm's length (0.2° of letter height at 58 cm), and a line above the chat says where and when the scene is and its light ("Recife · Sunday 15 July 1990, 18:20 · nautical twilight") instead of your device's clock; scrolling the box no longer pulls the page behind it.
 - The text box has a visual novel's look by default ("The box's look: night"): a see-through dark panel in white corner brackets under a pink name tag, white spoken words and light blue narration, the buttons as small pills under it, and a blinking ▼ when the line has finished appearing. "page" keeps the page's own look.
 - The first time a character with a character card of their own speaks, a card crosses the screen with their name and trade (or age), as visual novels introduce people; the story's time and place sit in a badge in the corner, and the box's buttons are words along its bottom edge.
-- Characters speak their lines aloud as they type (setting "Voices: natural", the default): each voice is computed from the speaker's body (the pitch their vocal folds give, their throat's length, their pace), applied to the averaged way of speaking of 16 people talking freely, so a tall man sounds low, a small child high and slow, and any body gives its own voice. It needs `voices/averaged-person.bin` (handed over separately; see voices/README.md) and downloads the speech model once (165 MB); the first line of a session waits for it (about 8 s on a desktop), a tap shows a waiting line at once. Narration and whispers stay silent. "robotic" speaks with eSpeak NG; "blips" gives one tone a syllable; "off" silences them.
+- Characters speak their lines aloud as they type (setting "Voices: natural", the default): each voice is computed from the speaker's body (the pitch their vocal folds give, their throat's length, their pace), applied to the averaged way of speaking of 16 people talking freely, so a tall man sounds low, a small child high and slow, and any body gives its own voice. It needs `voices/averaged-person.bin` (handed over separately; see voices/README.md) and downloads the speech model once (165 MB); the first line of a session waits for it (about 8 s on a desktop), a tap shows a waiting line at once. Narration and whispers stay silent. A character's feeling (from the Appraise tool, or a voice mark in the line) colours their voice by its strength: anger high and strained, sadness and tenderness narrow and slow, with `voices/emotion-<feeling>.bin` (handed over separately) in place. "robotic" speaks with eSpeak NG; "blips" gives one tone a syllable; "off" silences them.
 - One thing on screen at a time: with **Text box** on (the default), the chat shows one box at the foot of the screen under the name of whoever says it. A new box starts wherever said words begin or end, and a long stretch goes on in the next box. Said words are black (white on the dark page) with SPEAKING, or WHISPERING, at the plate's right; everything else is blue and belongs to your character, with THINKING when you work something out. Letters appear one by one, said words at the speaker's pace and the rest at reading pace; a tap shows the rest at once, then the next box. "Auto" moves on by itself once a box has been read, ‹ or ← goes back, and "Log" shows the whole chat. The narrator names who says each quotation, so the plate can show it; in your own lines, the person acting just before a quotation says it.
 - Words that name what the engine decided are marked in gold, as important words are in yellow in a visual novel. **Page colours** offers the light page or a dark one (white, light blue and gold on black), and **The speaker's picture** can show the character card's picture large above the box.
 - **Music**: add your own songs in the settings. Each is measured as you add it (tempo, loudness, brightness, key), and the song nearest the feelings in the scene plays: your line marked sad brings a calm song in a minor key, a character's joy a lively one in a major key. Give a song a set named after a character or a place, and it becomes their theme. When several feelings are in the scene, the strongest counts 90% and the newest 10%. A song stays while the scene stays in its area, unless another song is clearly nearer, and the next one fades in over 4 seconds. The ♪ button turns the music on and off.
